.Dd 2025-09-30
.Dt SSL_GET_CONN_CLOSE_INFO 3ossl
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm SSL_get_conn_close_info ,
.Nm SSL_CONN_CLOSE_FLAG_LOCAL ,
.Nm SSL_CONN_CLOSE_FLAG_TRANSPORT ,
.Nm OSSL_QUIC_ERR_NO_ERROR ,
.Nm OSSL_QUIC_ERR_INTERNAL_ERROR ,
.Nm OSSL_QUIC_ERR_CONNECTION_REFUSED ,
.Nm OSSL_QUIC_ERR_FLOW_CONTROL_ERROR ,
.Nm OSSL_QUIC_ERR_STREAM_LIMIT_ERROR ,
.Nm OSSL_QUIC_ERR_STREAM_STATE_ERROR ,
.Nm OSSL_QUIC_ERR_FINAL_SIZE_ERROR ,
.Nm OSSL_QUIC_ERR_FRAME_ENCODING_ERROR ,
.Nm OSSL_QUIC_ERR_TRANSPORT_PARAMETER_ERROR ,
.Nm OSSL_QUIC_ERR_CONNECTION_ID_LIMIT_ERROR ,
.Nm OSSL_QUIC_ERR_PROTOCOL_VIOLATION ,
.Nm OSSL_QUIC_ERR_INVALID_TOKEN ,
.Nm OSSL_QUIC_ERR_APPLICATION_ERROR ,
.Nm OSSL_QUIC_ERR_CRYPTO_BUFFER_EXCEEDED ,
.Nm OSSL_QUIC_ERR_KEY_UPDATE_ERROR ,
.Nm OSSL_QUIC_ERR_AEAD_LIMIT_REACHED ,
.Nm OSSL_QUIC_ERR_NO_VIABLE_PATH ,
.Nm OSSL_QUIC_ERR_CRYPTO_ERR_BEGIN ,
.Nm OSSL_QUIC_ERR_CRYPTO_ERR_END ,
.Nm OSSL_QUIC_ERR_CRYPTO_ERR ,
.Nm OSSL_QUIC_LOCAL_ERR_IDLE_TIMEOUT
.Nd get information about why a QUIC connection was closed
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In openssl/ssl.h
.Fd #define SSL_CONN_CLOSE_FLAG_LOCAL
.Fd #define SSL_CONN_CLOSE_FLAG_TRANSPORT
.Ft typedef struct ssl_conn_close_info_st {
     uint64_t error_code, frame_type;
     char     *reason;
     size_t   reason_len;
     uint32_t flags;
 } SSL_CONN_CLOSE_INFO;

 int
.Fo SSL_get_conn_close_info
.Fa "SSL *ssl"
.Fa "SSL_CONN_CLOSE_INFO *info"
.Fa "size_t info_len"
.Fc
.Fd #define OSSL_QUIC_ERR_NO_ERROR                  0x00
.Fd #define OSSL_QUIC_ERR_INTERNAL_ERROR            0x01
.Fd #define OSSL_QUIC_ERR_CONNECTION_REFUSED        0x02
.Fd #define OSSL_QUIC_ERR_FLOW_CONTROL_ERROR        0x03
.Fd #define OSSL_QUIC_ERR_STREAM_LIMIT_ERROR        0x04
.Fd #define OSSL_QUIC_ERR_STREAM_STATE_ERROR        0x05
.Fd #define OSSL_QUIC_ERR_FINAL_SIZE_ERROR          0x06
.Fd #define OSSL_QUIC_ERR_FRAME_ENCODING_ERROR      0x07
.Fd #define OSSL_QUIC_ERR_TRANSPORT_PARAMETER_ERROR 0x08
.Fd #define OSSL_QUIC_ERR_CONNECTION_ID_LIMIT_ERROR 0x09
.Fd #define OSSL_QUIC_ERR_PROTOCOL_VIOLATION        0x0A
.Fd #define OSSL_QUIC_ERR_INVALID_TOKEN             0x0B
.Fd #define OSSL_QUIC_ERR_APPLICATION_ERROR         0x0C
.Fd #define OSSL_QUIC_ERR_CRYPTO_BUFFER_EXCEEDED    0x0D
.Fd #define OSSL_QUIC_ERR_KEY_UPDATE_ERROR          0x0E
.Fd #define OSSL_QUIC_ERR_AEAD_LIMIT_REACHED        0x0F
.Fd #define OSSL_QUIC_ERR_NO_VIABLE_PATH            0x10
.Ft /* Inclusive range for handshake-specific errors. */
 #define OSSL_QUIC_ERR_CRYPTO_ERR_BEGIN          0x0100
 #define OSSL_QUIC_ERR_CRYPTO_ERR_END            0x01FF

 #define
.Fo OSSL_QUIC_ERR_CRYPTO_ERR
.Fa X
.Fc
.Fd #define OSSL_QUIC_LOCAL_ERR_IDLE_TIMEOUT
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Fn SSL_get_conn_close_info
function provides information about why and how a QUIC connection was
closed.
.Pp
Connection closure information is written to
.Em *info ,
which must be non-NULL.
.Fa info_len
must be set to
.Qo Li sizeof(*info) Qc .
.Pp
The following fields are set:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Em error_code
This is a 62-bit QUIC error code.
It is either a 62-bit application error code (if
.Sy SSL_CONN_CLOSE_FLAG_TRANSPORT
not set in
.Em flags )
or a 62-bit standard QUIC transport error code (if
.Sy SSL_CONN_CLOSE_FLAG_TRANSPORT
is set in
.Em flags Ns ).
.It Em frame_type
If
.Sy SSL_CONN_CLOSE_FLAG_TRANSPORT
is set, this may be set to a QUIC frame type number which caused the
connection to be closed.
It may also be set to 0 if no frame type was specified as causing the
connection to be closed.
If
.Sy SSL_CONN_CLOSE_FLAG_TRANSPORT
is not set, this is set to 0.
.It Em reason
If non-NULL, this is intended to be a UTF-8 textual string briefly
describing the reason for connection closure.
The length of the reason string in bytes is given in
.Em reason_len .
While, if non-NULL, OpenSSL guarantees that this string will be zero
terminated, consider that this buffer may originate from the (untrusted)
peer and thus may also contain zero bytes elsewhere.
Therefore, use of
.Em reason_len
is recommended.
.Pp
While it is intended as per the QUIC protocol that this be a UTF-8
string, there is no guarantee that this is the case for strings received
from the peer.
.It Sy SSL_CONN_CLOSE_FLAG_LOCAL
If
.Em flags
has
.Sy SSL_CONN_CLOSE_FLAG_LOCAL
set, connection closure was locally triggered.
This could be due to an application request (e.g.
if
.Sy SSL_CONN_CLOSE_FLAG_TRANSPORT
is unset), or (if
.Em SSL_CONN_CLOSE_FLAG_TRANSPORT
is set) due to logic internal to the QUIC implementation (for example,
if the peer engages in a protocol violation, or an idle timeout occurs).
.Pp
If unset, connection closure was remotely triggered.
.It Sy SSL_CONN_CLOSE_FLAG_TRANSPORT
If
.Em flags
has
.Sy SSL_CONN_CLOSE_FLAG_TRANSPORT
set, connection closure was triggered for QUIC protocol reasons.
Otherwise, connection closure was triggered by the local or remote
application.
.El
.Pp
The
.Sy OSSL_QUIC_ERR
macro definitions provide the QUIC transport error codes as defined by
RFC 9000. The
.Fn OSSL_QUIC_ERR_CRYPTO_ERR
macro can be used to convert a TLS alert code into a QUIC transport
error code by mapping it into the range reserved for such codes by RFC
9000. This range begins at
.Sy OSSL_QUIC_ERR_CRYPTO_ERR_BEGIN
and ends at
.Sy OSSL_QUIC_ERR_CRYPTO_ERR_END
inclusive.
.Sh NON-STANDARD TRANSPORT ERROR CODES
Some conditions which can cause QUIC connection termination are not
signalled on the wire and therefore do not have standard error codes.
OpenSSL indicates these errors via
.Fn SSL_get_conn_close_info
by setting
.Sy SSL_CONN_CLOSE_FLAG_TRANSPORT
and using one of the following error values.
These codes are specific to OpenSSL, and cannot be sent over the wire,
as they are above 2**62.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Sy OSSL_QUIC_LOCAL_ERR_IDLE_TIMEOUT
The connection was terminated immediately due to the idle timeout
expiring.
.El
.Sh RETURN VALUES
.Fn SSL_get_conn_close_info
returns 1 on success and 0 on failure.
This function fails if called on a QUIC connection SSL object which has
not yet been terminated.
It also fails if called on a QUIC stream SSL object or a non-QUIC SSL
object.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr SSL_shutdown_ex 3
.Sh HISTORY
This function was added in OpenSSL 3.2.
